          }
        ],
        "readouts": [
          {
            "id": "tfKey",
            "label": {
              "en": "key point (h, k)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": "—",
            "colorClass": "amber"
          }
        ],
        "equationDisplay": {
          "id": "tfEq",
          "value": "g(x) = (x)²",
          "style": "font-size:.84rem; padding:9px 11px; background:var(--paper-2); border-radius:8px"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 4.A — Describe the transformation",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>How is \\( y=(x-3)^2+2 \\) related to \\( y=x^2 \\)?</p><p>Matching \\( a=1,\\ h=3,\\ k=2 \\): the parabola shifts right \\( 3 \\) and up \\( 2 \\), placing the vertex at \\( (3,2) \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 4.B — Domain",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Find the domain of \\( f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1} \\).</p><p>The radicand must be non-negative: \\( x-1\\geq 0 \\), so the domain is \\( [1,\\infty) \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Functions and their transformations",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>A function assigns exactly one output to each input. Transformations shift, reflect, stretch, or compress the graph of a parent function.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "figure",
        "figure": {
          "type": "fn",
          "title": "Function Transformation: Reflection and Shifts",
          "xrange": [-4, 6],
          "yrange": [-5, 6],
          "fns": [
            { "fn": "x^2", "color": "var(--fig-muted)", "label": "f(x) = x²" },
            { "fn": "-(x-3)^2 + 4", "color": "var(--fig-indigo-2)", "label": "g(x) = -(x-3)² + 4" }
          ],
          "points": [
            { "x": 0, "y": 0, "label": "(0,0)" },
            { "x": 3, "y": 4, "label": "Vertex (3,4)" }
          ]
        },
        "caption": {
          "en": "Transformation from parent $f(x) = x^2$ to $g(x) = -(x-3)^2 + 4$: reflected across the $x$-axis, shifted right $3$ units, and shifted up $4$ units.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Vertical shift",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( y=f(x)+k \\): up \\( k \\) units",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Horizontal shift",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( y=f(x-h) \\): right \\( h \\) units",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Reflection",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( y=-f(x) \\): over \\( x \\)-axis; \\( y=f(-x) \\): over \\( y \\)-axis",
              "ar": null
            },
            "wide": true
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Stretch/compress",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( y=af(x) \\): vertical; \\( y=f(bx) \\): horizontal",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 4.A — Describing transformations",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Describe the transformation from \\( y=x^2 \\) to \\( y=-(x-3)^2+4 \\).</p><p>Right 3, reflect over \\( x \\)-axis, up 4. Vertex at \\( (3,4) \\), opens downward.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠ Common traps — Inside vs outside transformations & domain limits",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Counter-intuitive horizontal shifts:</strong> Inputs inside the parentheses operate in reverse: \\( f(x - c) \\) shifts RIGHT by \\( c \\), and \\( f(x + c) \\) shifts LEFT by \\( c \\). Outside shifts \\( f(x) + c \\) move UP as expected.</p><p><strong>Domain exclusions:</strong> Two universal algebraic rules dictate real domain boundaries: (1) denominators cannot equal zero (\\( \\frac{1}{g(x)} \\implies g(x) \\neq 0 \\)), and (2) expressions under even radicals must be non-negative (\\( \\sqrt{g(x)} \\implies g(x) \\ge 0 \\)).</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0\"><strong>Composition is not commutative:</strong> \\( (f \\circ g)(x) = f(g(x)) \\) means evaluate \\( g \\) first and plug into \\( f \\). In general, \\( f(g(x)) \\neq g(f(x)) \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Exam practice — Composite function and domain",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Problem:</strong> Given \\( f(x) = \\dfrac{2}{x - 1} \\) and \\( g(x) = \\sqrt{x + 5} \\), find (a) the value of \\( (f \\circ g)(4) \\), and (b) the domain of \\( g(x) \\).</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><p>1. Evaluate the inner function first: \\( g(4) = \\sqrt{4 + 5} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\).</p><p>2. Substitute into \\( f \\):</p>\\[ (f \\circ g)(4) = f(g(4)) = f(3) = \\frac{2}{3 - 1} = \\frac{2}{2} = 1. \\]<p>3. Domain of \\( g(x) = \\sqrt{x + 5} \\): The radicand must be non-negative:</p>\\[ x + 5 \\ge 0 \\implies x \\ge -5 \\quad\\text{or in interval notation } [-5, \\infty). \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
